<h1 style=3D"font-size:28px;line-height:1.25;margin:0 0 8px;">Why small team=
s ship faster</h1>
<p style=3D"font-size:14px;color:#666;margin:0 0 24px;">Issue #112 &middot; 7=
 min read</p>
<p style=3D"font-size:18px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 18px;">Every few month=
s someone asks why a team of four can out-ship a department of forty. The ans=
wer is rarely talent. It is almost always the cost of coordination. When the =
people who decide are the people who build, a decision takes an afternoon ins=
tead of a quarter.</p>
<p style=3D"font-size:18px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 18px;">This week we lo=
ok at three habits that keep small teams fast as they grow: writing decisions=
 down, keeping ownership narrow, and saying no to meetings that exist only t=
o share status. None of these are new ideas, but they are easy to lose.</p>
<h2 style=3D"font-size:22px;margin:28px 0 8px;">1. Decisions are written, no=
t remembered</h2>
<p style=3D"font-size:18px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 18px;">A short decisi=
on record, a paragraph or two, saves hours of re-litigating the same choice. =
It also lets new people understand why things are the way they are without =
booking a call.</p>
<h2 style=3D"font-size:22px;margin:28px 0 8px;">2. Ownership stays narrow</=
h2>
<p style=3D"font-size:18px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 18px;">When everyone =
owns a thing, nobody does. Small teams give each area one clear owner who ca=
n say yes without asking permission, and who is expected to say no often.</=
p>
